/F1 13 Tf
(ABSTRACT) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 50 638 Tm
() Tj
1 0 0 1 50 622 Tm
(Serverless computing paradigms are increasingly being extended from centralized cloud) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 606 Tm
(platforms to geographically distributed edge infrastructure. This paper addresses the) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 590 Tm
(function placement optimization problem in edge-cloud continuum environments, where) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 574 Tm
(latency-sensitive workloads must be dynamically allocated across heterogeneous computing) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 558 Tm
(nodes. We formulate the placement decision as a constrained optimization problem and) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 542 Tm
(propose a reinforcement learning-based scheduler that reduces average end-to-end latency) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 526 Tm
(by 41 percent compared to round-robin baselines while maintaining resource utilization) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 510 Tm
(above 82 percent. Evaluation on a 12-node testbed with realistic microservice workloads) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 494 Tm
(validates the practical applicability of our approach.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 478 Tm
() Tj
1 0 0 1 50 462 Tm
/F1 13 Tf
(Keywords: Serverless Computing, Edge Computing, Function Placement, Reinforcement Learning, Latency Optimization, Microservices) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 50 446 Tm
() Tj
1 0 0 1 50 430 Tm
/F1 13 Tf
(1. INTRODUCTION) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 50 414 Tm
(The evolution of cloud computing toward edge-cloud continuum architectures demands new) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 398 Tm
(approaches to workload orchestration. Traditional serverless platforms such as AWS Lambda) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 382 Tm
(and Azure Functions operate within centralized data centers, introducing latency penalties) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 366 Tm
(for geographically distributed applications. Edge computing promises sub-10ms response) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 350 Tm
(times by processing data closer to its source, but heterogeneous resource availability and) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 334 Tm
(dynamic demand patterns complicate scheduling decisions.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 318 Tm
() Tj
1 0 0 1 50 302 Tm
/F1 13 Tf
(2. PROBLEM FORMULATION) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 50 286 Tm
(We model the edge-cloud continuum as a weighted graph G = \(V, E\) where vertices represent) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 270 Tm
(computing nodes with heterogeneous CPU, memory, and GPU capacities, and edges represent) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 254 Tm
(network links with measured latency and bandwidth characteristics. The function placement) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 238 Tm
(problem seeks to assign incoming serverless invocations to computing nodes that minimize) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 222 Tm
(aggregate response latency subject to resource capacity constraints and data locality) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 206 Tm
(preferences.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 190 Tm
() Tj
1 0 0 1 50 174 Tm
/F1 13 Tf
(3. PROPOSED APPROACH) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 50 158 Tm
(Our reinforcement learning scheduler employs a Proximal Policy Optimization agent that) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 142 Tm
(observes system state vectors comprising current node utilizations, pending request) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 126 Tm
(queues, and historical latency measurements. The action space corresponds to node) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 110 Tm
(selection decisions, and the reward signal combines negative latency with resource balance) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 94 Tm
(penalties. We train the agent using simulated workload traces derived from Azure Function) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 78 Tm
(execution logs.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 62 Tm
() Tj
